.lua
book.chapter(5, "Bindings, scope, and program units")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Definitions describe relationships"
.pp
A binding associates a name with an expression's value. The clearest definition
marker is :=. The parser also accepts = in definition contexts, but using :=
consistently makes a binding visually different from a record field or a
comparison. A val prefix is useful inside structures and when attaching an
inline type annotation. Parameters written between a function name and its
definition marker turn the right-hand expression into a function body.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[base := 40
add :: Int -> Int -> Int
add x y := x + y
val answer : Int := add base 2
Test.equal "annotated binding" answer 42]=], "Value, function, signature, and inline annotation")
.endlua
.pp
The separate signature uses a double colon; the inline val annotation uses a
single colon. These are not interchangeable decorations. The signature states
the contract for a named definition, while the annotation constrains the value
being bound at that location. A useful signature documents the domain of an
operation rather than merely restating a type the reader could infer from a
literal. Public functions and module boundaries benefit most from that clarity.
.sh 2 "Immutability and shadowing"
.pp
Language bindings are immutable. Introducing another binding with the same
spelling shadows or redefines a name in a later scope; it is not assignment to
a mutable variable. Local bindings make the difference especially clear. The
outer value remains available after a nested expression introduces its own
name. Immutable data can still contain a foreign handle whose referenced
resource changes, so binding immutability and resource immutability must be
discussed separately.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[factor := 10
scaled := let factor := 3 in factor * 7
Test.equal "local shadow" scaled 21
Test.equal "outer binding" factor 10
make_adder amount := fn value -> amount + value
add_ten := make_adder factor
Test.equal "captured input" (add_ten 2) 12]=], "Scope and captured values")
.endlua
.pp
The interpreter resolves checked top-level references through binding cells.
Existing functions retain the references annotated during checking rather
than promising that every later REPL redefinition dynamically changes old
closures. That behavior is useful for stable lexical reasoning. It also means
that interactive redefinition is not a general dependency-rebuilding system.
If you want a computation to use new inputs, pass them explicitly or rebuild
the relevant functions in a fresh session.
.sh 2 "Scope is a design tool"
.pp
Keep a helper local when its meaning depends on one algorithm's invariant.
Promote it to a module member when it has an independent contract that other
code should share. Export it through a signature only when clients ought to
depend on that contract. This progression prevents accidental public APIs:
names that happened to be convenient during implementation should not all
become promises to downstream users.
.pp
A name also carries explanatory information. An expression such as item_count
communicates a role that a placeholder cannot. Tacit notation remains valuable
for short functions, but it does not remove the need for meaningful module and
function names. In a compiler pass, names such as free_variables, emit_term,
and check_operand explain relationships between stages. A collection of short
helpers with opaque names can be harder to understand than one well-structured
named definition.
.sh 2 "Programs are processed in checked units"
.pp
A submitted file is not a database transaction. The session parses and checks
program units, then executes successful work. If a later unit fails, earlier
successful definitions or external effects may remain. An import can read
another file, a primitive can mutate a table, and printing can already have
written bytes. Recovery restores interpreter bookkeeping where appropriate;
it cannot erase bytes from an external stream or undo arbitrary native code.
.lua
book.algorithm("Reason about a multi-unit submission", {
    "Read and classify each source unit.",
    "Check the unit's bindings and type relationships.",
    "Execute accepted work and retain its definitions.",
    "On a later error, report the failing span.",
    "Do not infer rollback of prior definitions or external effects.",
    "For a clean experiment, create a fresh session and run the corrected source."
})
.endlua
.pp
This matters when writing reloadable modules. Prefer top-level definitions
that build pure values and functions. Put file output, mutation, and host
interaction behind explicit entry functions. A development driver can then
reload definitions without also repeating a deployment action. A test can
construct the same module in a new process without depending on a previous
attempt having partially succeeded.
.sh 2 "Signatures and generalization"
.pp
MetaML has rank-one polymorphism. A general identity binding can be instantiated
at an integer use and a string use in the same program. That does not mean
every function parameter is itself universally quantified at every use.
Separate the polymorphism of a bound value from the type of one argument
supplied to another function. The type-inference chapter develops this
distinction through instantiation and unification rather than treating type
variables as untyped runtime boxes.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[identity :: 'a -> 'a
identity x := x
Test.equal "two instantiations" (identity 7, identity "seven") (7, "seven")
apply f x := f x
Test.equal "one application" (apply identity true) true]=], "A polymorphic binding used at different types")
.endlua
.pp
Explicit signatures are also useful for typed concatenation. The same
juxtaposition syntax can represent application or supported composition, and
the checker uses types to choose. An annotation narrows the intended relation
before runtime. When a definition becomes difficult to explain without
referring to a hidden inference decision, consider using an explicit pipeline
or composition operator as well as a signature. Readability is part of the
interface, not merely an aesthetic preference after the program works.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Inspect binding elaboration and Session evaluation in MetaML.hpp, then compare
the corresponding routines in MetaML.d. The language's own standard-library
modules demonstrate the progression from private helpers to sealed public
interfaces. As an exercise, write a pure function that builds a report string
and a separate function that prints it. Explain how this separation changes
the consequences of reloading a file. Then add an explicit type to the report
builder and check it at the prompt without printing anything.
